{"be bothered":{"id":"f99f9ef9-f8d1-4fe8-890b-6f876c91d7fb","word":"be bothered","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"have enthusiasm","word":"estar animado"}],"pos":"verb","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"I can't be bothered to clean the house today.","bold_text_offsets":[[8,19]],"type":"example","id":"f5ffbdcc-27b7-4a0f-8394-6cbb9c5d22a3"},{"text":"He can't be bothered drying all those dishes.","bold_text_offsets":[[9,20]],"type":"example","id":"065cd0d3-f346-4fb7-ab37-41c5e04bdacf"},{"text":"An egg and rasher of bacon for breakfast supply quite enough nitrogenous food for the day. Sometimes I have a treat. A cauliflower, etc. But generally I can't be bothered.","ref":"1986, Vladimir Naumovich Zharkov, translated by William B. Hubbard, Interior Structure of the Earth and Planets, CRC Press, →ISBN, page 273:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[159,170]],"id":"6514cfd3-1359-4310-953f-6f6157045280"},{"text":"This takes effort. It can be a lot of work. So, you see, magick is not an easy short-cut to achieving something because you can't be bothered to get it any other way.","ref":"1991, Raymond Buckland, Scottish Witchcraft: The History and Magick of the Picts, Llewellyn Worldwide, →ISBN, page 81:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[130,141]],"id":"fa0be041-581e-4345-9f97-0373732ce633"},{"text":"I've been using a computer instead of a typewriter for four years now, but I can't speak the language at all. I don't need it, and I can't be bothered unless it's going to be useful. The only kind of mouse I recognize is the four-legged variety, and I can't tell a bit from a byte.","ref":"1992, Victoria Branden, In Defence of Plain English: The Decline and Fall of Literacy in Canada, Dundurn, →ISBN, page 88:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[139,150]],"id":"f35ec9c7-9464-4e5c-972d-7367bc7a3bc0"}],"categories":["English entries with incorrect language header","English lemmas","English multiword terms","English negative polarity items","English predicates","English terms with quotations","English terms with usage examples","English verbs","Entries with translation boxes","Pages with 1 entry","Pages with entries","Terms with Danish translations","Terms with Finnish translations","Terms with Irish translations","Terms with Italian translations","Terms with Pennsylvania German translations","Terms with Portuguese translations","Terms with Swedish translations"],"raw_glosses":["(chiefly in the negative) To have the enthusiasm (to do something)."],"glosses":["To have the enthusiasm (to do something)."],"raw_tags":["in the negative"],"id":"6a6b27b6-2bee-41a5-b331-59569dbc867d"}],"related":[],"synonyms":[]},"be born yesterday":{"id":"c09caa63-2cf4-4e62-9f3c-3a038f88f845","word":"be born yesterday","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"to be inexperienced","tags":["masculine"],"word":"ser nascido ontem"}],"pos":"verb","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"I was not born yesterday, you know. I have done this before!","bold_text_offsets":[[2,5],[10,24]],"type":"example","id":"299f956c-5d05-419f-aa8c-990e67b299b5"},{"text":"\"Do you think,\" he added, with an ironical grin, \"that you'll go for to gammon me into that air! I'm hinnocent, I know, but I wasn't born yesterday exactly.\"","ref":"1840, Henry Cockton, chapter 60, in The Life and Adventures of Valentine Vox, the Ventriloquist, page 508:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[126,147]],"id":"e4e59564-be18-445e-bfaf-a00d20f77dae"},{"text":"\"Say, do I look like I was born yesterday? See any green in my eye, Cactus Center?\"","ref":"1915, William MacLeod Raine, chapter 6, in Steve Yeager:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[23,41]],"id":"69f3828e-d2bb-46c2-9808-5aa49855c05a"},{"text":"\"Compared to me, you were born yesterday.\"","ref":"1998, Gwyn Hyman Rubio, Icy Sparks, page 155:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[21,40]],"id":"6a2f10bb-9258-4806-9646-922c474e48fe"},{"text":"If you don't know important things about history, then it's as if you were born yesterday.","ref":"2005, Howard Zinn, Donaldo Pereira Macedo, Howard Zinn on Democratic Education, page 69:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[70,89]],"id":"128509f6-0228-4428-8e32-df08a8bd2ace"}],"categories":["English entries with incorrect language header","English informal terms","English lemmas","English multiword terms","English negative polarity items","English stative verbs","English terms with quotations","English terms with usage examples","English verbs","Entries with translation boxes","Pages with 1 entry","Pages with entries","Quotation templates to be cleaned","Requests for review of Norwegian translations","Terms with Dutch translations","Terms with Finnish translations","Terms with French translations","Terms with German translations","Terms with Hungarian translations","Terms with Italian translations","Terms with Norwegian translations","Terms with Polish translations","Terms with Portuguese translations","Terms with Russian translations","Terms with Scottish Gaelic translations","Terms with Spanish translations","Terms with Swedish translations"],"links":[["new","new"],["naive","naive"],["innocent","innocent"],["inexperienced","inexperienced"],["deceived","deceived"]],"synonyms":[{"word":"fall off the turnip truck"},{"word":"be born last week"},{"word":"come down in the last shower"}],"raw_glosses":["(informal, stative, chiefly in the negative) To be new, naive, innocent, inexperienced, or easily deceived."],"glosses":["To be new, naive, innocent, inexperienced, or easily deceived."],"tags":["informal","no-past-participle","no-present-participle","stative"],"raw_tags":["in the negative"],"id":"c0e998e7-3113-48f5-8328-f0b4b60889f7"}],"related":[{"word":"born at night but not last night"},{"word":"not one's first rodeo"},{"word":"today years old"}],"synonyms":[]},"be born":{"id":"4cdeadf4-4183-4bea-a150-2835dc5a0237","word":"be born","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code